hat: A recurring internal audit where compliance staff sample production logs from Annex III point 1(a) systems to confirm every minimum field under Article 12(3) is populated, uncorrupted, and retrievable on demand. (34 words)\nHow: Auditors pull fifty random inference events per system each quarter, verify timestamps, reference-database identifiers, input-data matches, and natural-person verification records against deployment manifests and HR sign-off trails. (31 words)\nWhy: Demonstrable audit trails are the single fastest way to resolve a regulator's ad-hoc information request under Article 78 and protect the institution from enforcement proceedings. (26 words)\n\nTotal: 7 + 34 + 31 + 26 = 98 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Lifetime Technical Documentation Pack as Market Differentiator\" \nWhat: A living documentation package for each high-risk system covering general characteristics, algorithms, training data provenance, risk-management records, and post-market monitoring reports, kept current throughout the product's operational life per Recital 71. (35 words)\nHow: Each R&D project team maintains a versioned documentation repository linked to the logging infrastructure; a compliance reviewer signs off updates at every release cycle, and the pack is packaged as a deliverable for EU public-sector tender submissions. (38 words)\n\nThat's too long. Let me trim.\n\nTitle: \"Lifetime Documentation Pack as Tender Differentiator\" \nWhat: A living documentation package per high-risk system covering algorithms, training provenance, risk-management records, and post-market monitoring reports, updated at every release cycle throughout the product's operational life per Recital 71. (33 words)\nHow: Each project team maintains a versioned repository linked to its logging infrastructure; a compliance reviewer signs off updates per release, and the finished pack is bundled into EU public-sector tender responses as evidence of sustained regulatory alignment. (37 words