        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"Article 12 does not alter the scientific questions our lab pursues—our model architectures, training strategies, and intended purposes remain the same—but it does insert a non-negotiable engineering layer into every high-risk project's critical path: automatic lifetime logging with specific capture fields for Annex III 1(a) systems. From a resource-allocation standpoint, the smartest response is to treat this as an infrastructure and process investment rather than a per-project tax; if we retrofit our existing server clusters once and build a shared middleware hook, the marginal cost to each subsequent project drops sharply. The 'lifetime' qualifier in paragraph 1 also means storage and retention are recurring operational line-items that must appear in every grant budget from the first call, not as an end-of-project compliance scramble. For projects touching biometric identification under Annex III 1(a), we additionally need to redesign verification workflows so that the natural persons checking results (Article 14(5)) are captured as structured log entries, which touches our human-in-the-loop protocols rather than just our compute stack. Overall this is a meaningful but bounded adjustment: it changes *how* we build and sequence work more than *what* we set out to discover.\",\n  \"stance\": 35,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Retrofit existing lab servers for lifetime log capture\",\n      \"what\": \"We upgrade our current R&D compute and storage infrastructure to support continuous, automatic event logging for every high-risk system in active development, ensuring that from the moment a model enters validation through its full operational lifecycle, all relevant events are recorded without manual intervention.\",\n      \"how\": \"The IT team audits each existing server cluster for log-retention capacity, deploys timestamped audit daemons alongside our current monitoring stack, and establishes retention schedules aligned to system lifetimes rather than project deadlines.\",\n      \"why\": \"This converts a regulatory obligation into a one-time infrastructure upgrade that serves all concurrent projects simultaneously, protecting us from per-project compliance costs later.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Front-load compliance engineering into project scoping\",\n      \"what\": \"We revise our internal grant and project-charter templates so that every high-risk AI system proposal must include a dedicated line-item for logging architecture design, data-retention budgeting, and Annex III 1(a)-specific capture logic before the science team begins modelling work.\",\n      \"how\": \"The lab's programme office introduces a mandatory compliance-feasibility checkpoint at the feasibility-review stage, where engineers estimate storage, compute overhead, and verification-workflow changes (including naming of verifying persons under Article 14(5)) and adjust timelines accordingly.\",\n      \"why\": \"Embedding these costs at the scoping stage prevents mid-project scope bloat and keeps our funding applications realistic without sacrificing scientific ambition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Build a shared automatic-logging middleware layer\",\n      \"what\": \"We develop an internal library that wraps model inference pipelines with standardised event-capture hooks recording start/end timestamps, reference-database lookups, matched inputs, and verifier identities, so any high-risk project can adopt compliant logging without bespoke engineering.\",\n      \"how\": \"A three-person cross-project team maintains the middleware as a versioned internal asset with unit tests mapping each hook to Article 12(2)(a)–(c) and Annex III 1(a); projects adopt it during their integration sprint.\",\n      \"why\": \"This converts a recurring per-project compliance expense into a one-time R&D investment that compounds as our high-risk portfolio grows, freeing researchers to focus on mo