        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"Article 12's lifetime logging mandate is, from a research standpoint, an underappreciated instrument for studying long-term AI system behavior — something the field has wanted but rarely had because most deployments lack sustained observation windows. The four minimum Annex III paragraph-3 fields plus generic event hooks give us structured longitudinal data on edge-case emergence, distributional drift, and failure-mode evolution that no prior experimental paradigm could match. I would not abandon any open research question, but I would reweight my agenda to treat the compliance logging infrastructure as a primary scientific dataset rather than a mere regulatory by-product. The 'comprehensible documentation' standard in Recital 71 also nudges our methodology toward reproducibility norms that strengthen peer scrutiny of our published results. Net effect: same core questions, new data access, tighter methodological discipline.\",\n\"stance\": 54,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Longitudinal Drift Lab from Mandated Operational Logs\",\n\"what\": \"A standing research program treating Article 12-compliant operational logs as a continuous experimental dataset for studying performance degradation, population drift, and edge-case emergence across full system lifetimes.\",\n\"how\": \"Two postdocs build statistical pipelines ingesting the paragraph-3 minimum fields plus generic event hooks, running rolling comparisons of early versus late-lifetime behavior on fixed benchmark slices each quarter.\",\n\"why\": \"It converts a regulatory obligation into unique longitudinal reliability data that no other lab currently holds and directly advances the empirical science underpinning Article 72 post-market monitoring.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Open Anonymized Failure-Case Taxonomy Dataset\",\n\"what\": \"A curated public benchmark of anonymized failure events and edge-condition categories drawn from our logged operations, letting external researchers probe robustness hypotheses without needing their own high-risk deployment.\",\n\"how\": \"After ethics review strips all Article 14(5) verifier identifiers and remaining natural-person data, we release the residual records on Hugging Face in a versioned schema mirroring paragraph-3 fields, accompanied by a CC-BY-NC methods paper.\",\n\"why\": \"It extends our scientific reach beyond these walls by giving the AI-safety community empirical deployment data that no synthetic benchmark can reproduce.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Dual-Audience Reproducibility Template for High-Risk AI Research\",\n\"what\": \"A lab-internal documentation template structuring our training, validation, and risk-management write-ups to satisfy both Recital 71's clear-and-comprehensive standard and the reproducibility expectations of peer-reviewed ML venues.\",\n\"how\": \"I convene a small working group with two senior researchers to draft it over one quarter, pilot it on our next Annex III submission, then refine using reviewer and regulator feedback before codifying as standing policy.\",\n\"why\": \"It ensures our intellectual contributions are judged on scientific substance rather than lost in opaque methodology narratives, while closing the documentation gap Article 12 makes visible to peers and authorities alike.\"\n}\n]\n}",
