ataset about AI system degradation, distributional drift, and edge-case emergence. This is scientifically valuable for understanding long-term AI behavior — something the field has been calling for but rarely gets because most deployments don't have sustained observation windows.\n\nNow, my stance: I'm not going to say \"no change\" (0) because the regulation actually opens research opportunities and imposes documentation standards that affect how we publish and share findings. But I also won't go to 100 — the science questions I care about don't fundamentally change. The regulation is a constraint on HOW we build and document, not WHAT we study. However, it does shift some emphasis: I need to think more about long-term system behavior (because logs accumulate over lifetime), about reproducibility of training/validation processes (because they must be documented clearly), and about the scientific value of operational data (because the regulation guarantees its collection).\n\nI'd put my stance around 50-62. Let me settle on 54. It's a moderate adjustment — I'm not overhauling what research questions I pursue, but I am:\n- Restructuring how I document and present methodology (for intellectual rigor and compliance)\n- Adding a new sub-agenda around longitudinal system behavior analysis (leveraging the mandated logs as scientific data)\n- Adjusting publication strategy to align with \"comprehensible\" documentation standards\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about using the mandated logging infrastructure as a scientific instrument — a research program on long-term AI system degradation and drift detection, leveraging the operational logs that Article 12 guarantees will exist.\n\n2. Something about creating open, anonymized benchmark datasets derived from the logging requirements — contributing to broader scientific impact by sharing (anonymized) failure-mode taxonomies or edge-case catalogs that other researchers can use. This is different from #1 becau