ducible machine-learning operations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Detecting Substantial Modification in Evolving AI Models\",\n      \"what\": \"A study to formalise and algorithmically detect the moment a continuously learning or fine-tuned high-risk system crosses into what Article 12(2)(a) calls a substantial modification, distinguishing that threshold-crossing from benign statistical drift.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would construct a labelled corpus of incremental model updates with known ground-truth classification, train change-detection detectors over both weight-space and behaviour-space signatures, and validate the results against the risk-management documentation our compliance team already maintains for post-market surveillance under Article 72.\",\n      \"why\": \"The Act leaves 'substantial modification' underspecified, so establishing a principled, empirically grounded detection method positions the lab at the frontier of post-deployment ML science while giving regulators an evidence-based benchmark they can adopt.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Privacy-Preserving Logs for Biometric Identification Pipelines\",\n      \"what\": \"A technical investigation into how Article 12(3)'s mandatory recording of reference databases, matched input data, and verifier identities can be satisfied without retaining raw biometric records in plaintext, using cryptographic commitment or differential-privacy schemes.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would prototype a two-layer logging pipeline in which raw captures are immediately reduced to verifiable hashes and k-anonymity buckets at ingestion time, then stress-test the scheme against re-identification attacks while confirming that post-market monitoring queries remain fully answerable by our operations team.\",\n      \"why\": \"It resolves a live tension between traceability and data-minimisation principles for Annex III point 1(a) systems, producing both deployable tooling for partner clients and a peer-reviewed 