e calls and automatically records the reference database identifier, matched input data hashes, and verifier identities as first-class metadata alongside every prediction batch. \nHow: The team ships an internal library with context-managers around inference endpoints; a thin middleware intercepts each call, stamps the required fields per Article 12(3)(b)-(d), and flushes records to our event store via async batching. \nWhy: Prototyping new biometric models then requires zero additional compliance code from the researcher, preserving iteration speed while guaranteeing record completeness. \nTotal: 9 + 30 + 31 + 23 = 93 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: Validate Log Durability and Query Latency Over Simulated Lifetimes\nWhat: A dedicated systems-engineering workstream that stress-tests our log storage architecture against realistic multi-year retention scenarios, verifying we can retrieve any event within seconds while maintaining integrity across hardware migrations. How: We build a synthetic-data generator that produces ten years of high-volume inference logs at projected throughput, then run automated regression tests measuring p95 query latency, checksum integrity after simulated node failures, and schema-drift detection when model versions update mid-lifetime. Why: This de-risks the \"lifetime\" requirement before we commit to production deployments, ensuring our storage choice is technically viable rather than assumed.\n\nLet me count:\nTitle: Validate Log Durability and Query Latency Over Simulated Lifetimes \nWhat: A dedicated systems-engineering workstream that stress-tests our log storage architecture against realistic multi-year retention scenarios, verifying we can retrieve any event within seconds while maintaining integrity across hardware migrations. \nHow: We build a synthetic-data generator that produces ten years of high-volume inference logs at projected throughput, then run automated regression tests measuring p95 query latency, checksum integri