        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"From a resource-allocation standpoint, Article 12 does not prohibit any research line we currently pursue, but it converts what was previously an optional engineering nicety into a fixed per-project cost that must appear in every budget from day one: the system must technically allow automatic event logging over its full lifetime, and for Annex III 1(a) biometric-identification work the minimum field list (start/end timestamps, reference-database identifier, matched input data, verifier identity) adds storage, privacy-review, and workflow-documentation overhead that directly erodes per-project margins. The pragmatic response is not to shrink our scientific ambition but to build a shared logging backbone into the R&D infrastructure we already operate, so the compliance cost is amortised across all active and future high-risk projects rather than re-incurred team by team. I also need to audit the current portfolio honestly: a few projects where retroactive compliance would consume more budget than the remaining scientific yield justifies should be deferred or re-scoped before we lock in multi-year funding commitments. The lifetime dimension is the real constraint for me, because it turns a one-off development expense into a recurring operational line item that must survive beyond the grant cycle that funded the original research, so I need to secure a dedicated operations budget rather than burying it in project P&L.\",\n\"stance\": 58,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Centralised Lifetime Logging Middleware\",\n\"what\": \"Develop a reusable logging framework that auto-captures the Article 12(3) minimum fields for Annex III biometric-identification systems and generic event logs for all other high-risk work.\",\n\"how\": \"Dedicate a small engineering pod of three to four FTE over two quarters to build an API layer hooking into our existing ML pipeline, so every new system project inherits compliant logging by default rather 