or Annex III 1(a) systems specifically, there are granular minimum requirements.\n\nStance number: I'll say around 35-45. It's not a total overhaul because logging is something you can engineer into existing systems, but it does require meaningful changes to R&D processes - you need to design for loggability from the start rather than bolting it on. It also affects documentation practices and post-market monitoring workflows. I'll go with 40.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about engineering/logging architecture - a technical project to build logging infrastructure into system development. This is about the \"how we build\" aspect.\n\n2. Something about documentation lifecycle management - maintaining up-to-date technical documentation throughout the system's lifetime as Recital 71 emphasizes. This is about the \"what we maintain\" aspect.\n\n3. Something about post-market monitoring capability or compliance testing/certification readiness - perhaps a project to build internal audit/training infrastructure, or a project to develop compliance-as-code tools that automatically verify logging requirements are met before deployment. Or maybe a market-facing initiative like publishing compliance documentation standards to boost reputation.\n\nLet me make them genuinely different:\n1. Technical architecture: Embed automatic event-logging into the R&D pipeline as a design constraint (engineering focus)\n2. Documentation lifecycle: Create a living technical documentation system that tracks changes across the AI system's full lifetime (information management focus)\n3. Market/reputation: Develop an open compliance verification framework or toolkit that demonstrates logging readiness to regulators and clients (strategic/external-facing focus)\n\nLet me draft these now, keeping word counts between 60-110 words per proposal total across all four fields.\n\nLet me count carefully.\n\nProposal 1: \"Embed Loggability as a Design Constraint in R&D Pipelines\"\nTitle