        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"Article 12 does not ask us to change what science we pursue; it asks us to change how every pipeline run is instrumented and documented, so my first instinct is to treat logging as an infrastructure retrofit rather than a new research line. The resource-allocation question is where this bites: if we spin up a parallel 'compliance' team, we burn headcount that would otherwise advance the models themselves, whereas wiring structured event-emitters into the orchestration tools we already pay for keeps marginal cost low and reuses storage and version-control infrastructure that sits idle most of the week. The Annex III 1(a) specifics in paragraph 3 (operator identification, reference-database references) do mean a few data fields our current experiment trackers simply do not capture, so a modest schema extension is unavoidable. I would budget this as a two-quarter engineering sprint plus an ongoing documentation-generation process, funded from the existing R&D overhead line rather than requesting new grant money, which preserves our scientific ambition while meeting the letter of the regulation. The balance tilts toward 'absorb and adapt' rather than 'pivot,' because the regulation's substance is traceability and audit-readiness, not a redefinition of acceptable research questions.\",\n\"stance\": 32,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Retrofit Logging into Existing ML Pipelines\",\n\"what\": \"We instrument our current training and serving infrastructure so every run auto-captures the event logs Article 12(1) demands, including timestamps, reference databases checked, input-data matches, and verification-operator IDs. This is a thin middleware layer bolted onto frameworks we already use rather than a parallel system.\",\n\"how\": \"A two-sprint engineering effort wraps existing pipeline components with structured emitters writing to an append-only log store in our data platform; no new hardware is procured, only lifetime retention policies configured within storage we budgeted last quarter.\",\n\"why\": \"This converts what would otherwise be a pure compliance cost into a reusable reproducibility tool that also strengthens future funding pitches by demonstrating audit-ready R&D hygiene to evaluators.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Compliance-as-Code for Technical File Generation\",\n\"what\": \"We build an internal tool that auto-generates the Article 12 technical documentation—general characteristics, algorithms, data provenance, risk-management records—directly from structured experiment metadata our researchers already log. The output is a living document regenerated on every pipeline run.\",\n\"how\": \"Researchers tag experiments with a lightweight YAML schema; a CI hook in our existing Git infrastructure parses those tags, renders the compliance file into a version-controlled repository, and flags any field that would fail a notified-body audit review.\",\n\"why\": \"This eliminates the manual documentation burden currently eating researcher hours and guarantees we never face an unexplained gap when a market-surveillance authority requests lifetime records.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Lightweight Long-Horizon Logging for Edge R&D\",\n\"what\": \"We run a focused research programme investigating how to satisfy Article 12's lifetime-logging mandate on resource-constrained deployments—edge sensors, long-running autonomous experiments—without prohibitive storage or bandwidth costs. The core question is scientific: what minimal event schema preserves full auditability?\",\n\"how\": \"A small PhD-funded team prototypes compressible, cryptographically chained log formats and benchmarks them against our existing cluster infrastructure; findings feed back into the Proposal 1 middleware as binding design constraints.\",\n\"why\": \"This positions the lab at the intersection of regulatory compliance and systems research, making Article 12 a fundable Horizon topic in AI-governance efficiency rather than an un