ound 30-40. It's not business as usual because there are specific technical requirements (automatic logging, lifetime retention, specific data fields for Annex III 1(a) systems), but it's also not a major overhaul. The lab is an R&D institution - the changes are about how we instrument and document our work, not about what science we pursue. Maybe 35.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\nProposal 1: Infrastructure/technical - retrofit existing ML pipeline with automatic logging capability\n- This is a technical engineering project to add log capture into the model training/serving infrastructure\n- Different because it's about the technical system itself\n\nProposal 2: Process/governance - create a \"compliance-as-code\" layer that auto-generates Article 12 documentation from experiment metadata\n- This is about workflow and tooling, not the models themselves\n- Different because it's about documentation generation and process\n\nProposal 3: Research/scientific - investigate lightweight logging architectures for resource-constrained R&D environments (e.g., edge deployment, long-running experiments) that meet Article 12 without prohibitive storage costs\n- This is a research question in itself, fitting the R&D mission\n- Different because it's forward-looking science that also serves compliance\n\nLet me draft these and count words carefully.\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Retrofit Experiment Tracing into Existing ML Pipelines\" (8 words) ✓\nWhat: We would instrument our current model-training and serving infrastructure so that every run automatically captures the event logs Article 12(1) demands, including start/end timestamps, reference databases checked, input-data matches, and verification-operator IDs where applicable. This means adding a thin logging middleware layer to frameworks we already use (PyTorch, TensorFlow, our internal orchestration tools).\nHow: A two-sprint engineering effort assigns a small team to wrap existing pipeline components with structured event 