        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"From the research side, Article 12 does not redirect what we study but it reframes several of our working assumptions: a model that ships without a native, interpretable event-recording layer is no longer 'complete,' and the lifetime-documentation requirement in Recital 71 means our validation story must be continuous rather than point-in-time. What excites me is that the logging mandates—especially the Annex III 1(a) specifics on input-match records and verifier identities—open genuinely novel questions at the intersection of privacy-preserving data capture, formal drift detection for 'substantial modification' triggers, and log interpretability as an evaluative metric in its own right. These are publishable, frontier-extending problems; treating them purely as engineering chores (as my Lab Director is inclined to) would waste their intellectual merit. That said, the core scientific agenda—building capable models—remains intact, so the adjustment is a redirection of perhaps a third of our validation-and-monitoring effort toward new methodological work rather than a reorientation of the programme.\",\n  \"stance\": 32,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Privacy-Preserving Compliance Logging for Biometric AI\",\n      \"what\": \"A research programme developing cryptographic and differential-privacy techniques that let us satisfy Article 12(3) logging mandates—recording input matches, reference-database identifiers, and verifier identities—without creating a re-identifiable surveillance dataset.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would collaborate with our applied-cryptography group to design secure-aggregation or homomorphic schemes for biometric-match logs, then validate them against both Article 12 traceability requirements and GDPR data-minimisation principles in controlled test deployments.\",\n      \"why\": \"It converts a compliance constraint into a publishable contribution at the intersection of privacy engineering