or 3 proposals that are genuinely different:\n\n1. A research project on privacy-preserving compliance logging - this addresses the tension between Article 12(3) requirements (log input data matches, reference DB identifiers) and GDPR/privacy concerns in biometric systems. This is a new technical research direction.\n\n2. Developing automated \"substantial modification\" detection frameworks - connecting to Article 12(2)(a). This is about defining and detecting when a system has changed enough to constitute a \"substantial modification,\" which requires novel methods in model drift, version control for neural networks, and continuous evaluation.\n\n3. Creating an open-source benchmark/suite for \"log interpretable\" AI systems - where the research contribution is developing metrics and methodologies that assess whether an AI system's logging is actually useful for traceability (not just present). This has broader scientific impact as it creates a standard for the field.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is about privacy engineering / cryptographic techniques for compliance data\n- #2 is about ML model monitoring, drift detection, and formal definitions of \"substantial modification\"\n- #3 is about evaluation methodology, benchmarking, and creating standards for the field\n\nYes, these are distinct: one is a technical/privacy problem, one is an ML-systems problem, one is a methodology/standards contribution.\n\nLet me write them out with proper word counts (60-110 words each across all four fields).\n\nProposal 1: Privacy-Preserving Compliance Logging for Biometric Systems\n- title: \"Privacy-Preserving Compliance Logging for Biometric AI\"\n- what: A research programme developing cryptographic and differential-privacy techniques that let us satisfy Article 12(3) logging mandates—recording input matches, reference-database identifiers, and verifier identities—without creating a re-identifiable surveillance dataset.\n- how: We would collaborate