 The reward is \"intellectual credibility\" and a peer-reviewed paper. The cost is significant R&D resources spent on what should be a software engineering task (implementing logs).\n\n    *   As Lab Director, I care about *efficiency*. Spending months or years developing a \"Formal Information-Theoretic Framework\" just to figure out which logs to keep for the EU AI Act feels like \"academic over-engineering.\"\n    *   Compliance needs to be achieved reliably and cost-effectively. A theoretical approach that prioritizes publication before implementation delays actual compliance and consumes high-value researcher time on a low-impact (from a scientific perspective) problem.\n    *   The promise of \"minimal\" logs is an efficiency gain, but the *cost* to find those minima is likely higher than the cost of slightly suboptimal logging.\n\n    *   Rationale: While theoretically elegant, this proposal over-engineers a compliance requirement into a research project, consuming scarce high-level theoretical resources for marginal gains in log efficiency. The priority should be pragmatic implementation over publishing a paper on regulatory minimalism.\n    *   Decision: Reject.\n    *   Promise: Low (maybe 20-30 because it does use existing groups and has some logic to it, but the ROI is poor).",
