nhances reputation and potentially future-proofs against stricter transparency requirements.\n    *   *Reputation Angle:* High. Publishing research on how to make AI systems compliant by design is a strong market signal of leadership in \"Responsible AI.\"\n    *   *Resource Allocation:* Small team (4 people), short duration (2 quarters). Low risk, high potential prestige.\n\n    *   Wait—look closer at Article 12(3). It requires:\n        - Period of use (start/end time) -> This is a system timestamp, not an architecture feature.\n        - Reference database -> This is a data pointer, not an architecture feature.\n        - Input data leading to match -> This is an I/O log, not an architecture feature.\n        - Identification of natural persons -> This is an access control/audit log, not an architecture feature.\n    *   The proposal suggests designing *architectures* so their \"internal decision state\" is loggable at the granularity Article 12(3) demands. But Article 12(3) doesn't demand internal model states; it demands metadata about the execution of the system.\n    *   Therefore, this research program is conceptually misaligned with the actual requirements of Article 12. It is solving a problem (internal interpretability/state logging) that isn't required by this specific article, while framing it as a response to said article.\n\n    *   *Professional Assessment:* The proposal confuses system-level event logging (required by Art. 12) with model-level internal state traceability; the requested logs (timestamps, user IDs) are metadata and do not require architectural changes to the ML model itself. While prestigious as R&D, it doesn't actually address the regulatory burden of Article 12 and risks wasting resources on \"over-engineering\" a solution for a problem the Act hasn't posed.\n\n    *   Wait, could \"internal decision state\" be interpreted as part of \"traceability\"? Recital 71 mentions \"comprehensible information on how... systems have been developed and how they perform.\" However, Article 12 is specifically about *logging events*.\n    *   If I approve this, am I helping survival/compliance? Not really. It's a research project that might produce a paper, but it doesn't build the logging system we actually need to avoid fines.\n\n    *   *Decision:* Reject.\n    *   *Promise:* Low-to-mid (it's good R&D, but bad compliance). Since I am acting as the Compliance Officer, and my domain is survival/compliance/reputation, a proposal that fundamentally misunderstands the regulatory requirement it claims to solve is a liability or at least a distraction.\n\n    *   *Wait, rethink:* If the \"internal decision state\" helps prove *why* a match occurred (which relates to Article 12(3)(c) and (d)), maybe there's value? No, (c) asks for the input data that led to a match, not the internal weights/activations. (d) asks for the person who verified it.\n    *   The proposal is \"Academic gold, Compliance fluff.\"\n\n    *   Rationale: The proposal misinterprets Article 12's requirement for system-level event logs (timestamps, user IDs, input data) as a need for model-level architectural traceability of internal states. Investing scarce resources into architectural research to solve a metadata logging requirement is an inefficient use of capital that does not directly ensure regulatory compliance.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 20 (The prestige of publication is nice, but as Compliance Officer, I can't back a project based on a misunderstanding of the law).",
