st about *recording* logs (the legal requirement), but *monitoring* them actively.\n    *   From a Dev Engineer's perspective, \"automatic recording\" is the baseline; \"automated monitoring\" is the professional way to implement it to avoid manual slogs during audits.\n    *   It transforms a passive record-keeping task into an active observability pipeline. This reduces technical debt and ensures that we aren't just dumping logs into a black hole (a common failure in R&D).\n\n    *   Rationale: Building a real-time monitoring pipeline for logs is technically straightforward using existing observability stacks and prevents the \"log dump\" problem, ensuring our recording capabilities actually serve a functional purpose.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85 (It's very promising because it aligns technical observability with regulatory needs, though slightly resource-heavy due to the team requirement).",
