cital 71) or if the \"gate\" fails, the institution faces regulatory non-compliance. However, the proposal specifically mentions a \"documented science-to-system transition criterion,\" which provides an audit trail for the compliance process itself.\n    *   From a *survival/reputation* standpoint: Stifling research is a risk to institutional survival (loss of talent, loss of edge). But regulatory fines are a massive risk to survival. A gated approach balances these.\n    *   Crucially, Article 12(3) requirements are for the *system*. If the prototype isn't yet \"the system\" being put into service, delayed full implementation is logical.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* The proposal aligns with a risk-based deployment lifecycle by ensuring full regulatory compliance at the point of market entry while avoiding unnecessary overhead during pure R&D. The inclusion of a documented transition gate and a shared SDK mitigates the risk of forgetting to implement logging before deployment.\n    *   *Decision:* Approve.\n    *   *Promise:* High, but not 100 because the \"deployment-readiness gate\" must be rigorously audited to prevent accidental leakages of non-compliant systems into production.\n\n    *   Check constraints: Rationale (1-2 sentences), decision (\"approve\"/\"reject\"), promise (0-100).\n    *   Echo ID: P1457.",
