        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2166\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Addressing the inherent tension between Article 12's record-keeping mandates and GDPR data minimization via HE/DP is technically ambitious but critical for building a viable production architecture that avoids massive security liabilities. The bounded six-month timeline and specific benchmarking deliverables provide sufficient guardrails to ensure this doesn't become an endless research sink.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 75\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nTechnical viability, speed/quality of prototyping.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping). High-risk AI systems must have automatic logging. Specifically, for biometric identification (Annex III 1(a)), logs must include start/end times, reference databases, input data that matched, and the person verifying results.\n\n        *   ID: P2166\n        *   Text: \"Privacy-Preserving Biometric Logging Research Seed.\"\n        *   Goal: Investigate differential privacy (DP) and homomorphic encryption (HE) to record required logs (input matches, personnel IDs) without persisting raw biometric data.\n        *   Plan: 6-month internal project, 8% discretionary budget, two postdocs in applied cryptography.\n        *   Outcome: Benchmarked deliverable vs Annex III specs; publishable R&D output; reduced GDPR exposure.\n\n    *   *Technical Viability:* Homomorphic encryption (HE) and Differential Privacy (DP) are mathematically sound but computationally expensive and complex to implement, especially for real-time biometric matching/logging. However, as an R&D institution, exploring these is within the scope of \"technical viability\" research.\n    *   *Speed/Quality of Prototyping:* This is a *research seed*. It doesn't provide an immediate solution but proposes a 6-month window to find one. From a prototyping perspective, relying on HE for logging might slow down the actual deployment of the AI system because it adds significant overhead and complexity to the pipeline.\n    *   *Regulatory Alignment:* The EU AI Act requires recording \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" (Art 12(3)(c)). If we encrypt/anonymize this via HE or DP, will regulators accept that as \"recording\"? If the goal is to avoid persisting *raw* biometric inputs while still satisfying the record-keeping requirement, it's a high-risk but high-reward technical gamble.\n    *   *Resource Commitment:* The prompt says approving means committing implementation resources. This proposal asks for budget and personnel (two postdocs) for research.\n\n    *   *Pros:* Solves the tension between AI Act record-keeping (keep data) and GDPR (minimize data). Could lead to a competitive technical advantage/publication.\n    *   *Cons:* High complexity. HE is often too slow for production logging in high-throughput systems. There's a risk that after 6 months, we find it's not viable, meaning we've wasted resources and still haven't implemented the mandatory Article 12 requirements.\n    *   *Engineering Perspective:* As a Dev Engineer focused on speed/quality of prototyping, I prefer solutions that *actually work* for deployment. However, in an R&D context, solving the \"privacy vs. compliance\" paradox is a critical technical hurdle. If we just store raw biometric data (the easy way), we create a massive security liability.\n\n    *   The proposal is structured as a research seed with a clear timeframe (6 months) and specific personnel.\n    *   It targets a very specific, difficult technical requirement of Article 12(3).\n    *   While HE/DP are hard, the alternative (raw storage) is a GDPR nightmare.\n    *   The deliverable is benchmarked against the actual specifications.\n\n    *Wait*, look at the persona: \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\"\n    Is this *viable*? It's an investigation into viability.\n    Does it help *speed/quality*? In the long run, a privacy-preserving log is higher quality than a raw log that creates legal risk. But in the short term, it doesn't provide a \"plug-and-play\" solution for the prototypes we need to ship.\n\n    However, given this is an R&D institution and the proposal is limited to 6 months with two postdocs, it is a reasonable engineering bet to avoid future technical debt/legal liability.\n\n    *   Rationale: Addressing the conflict between Article 12's data retention requirements and GDPR minimization via HE/DP is technically ambitious but essential for creating a viable, deployable production architecture for biometric systems. The limited timeframe and specific benchmarking goal make this an acceptable R&D risk to avoid massive future liability.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 75 (Strong, because it solves a core conflict, though the technical execution of HE is notoriously difficult).",
