        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer (R&D Institution).\nTechnical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping) for high-risk AI systems.\nScreen a specific proposal (P2045) independently based on my professional vantage point.\n\n        *   Rationale: 1-2 sentences (written first).\n        *   Decision: \"approve\" or \"reject\".\n        *   Promise: Integer 0-100.\nStrictly judge on merits; no quotas/comparisons.\n\n    *   *Text:* Unified Logging Middleware for Existing Systems. A single middleware layer between model-serving and storage that automatically captures, timestamps, and schema-normalizes inference events, input matches, and human verification actions for all high-risk deployments.\n    *   *Resources:* Two senior engineers, six-month sprint.\n    *   *Implementation:* Hook into existing pipelines, write to current time-series DB, no additional hardware cost.\n    *   *Goal:* Turn per-system burden into a shared infrastructure asset.\n\n    *   *Technical Viability:* High. A middleware approach for logging is standard architectural practice (Interceptors/Aspects/Middleware). Capturing timestamps and schema normalization ensures the \"comprehensible information\" and \"automatic recording of events\" required by Article 12.\n    *   *Speed/Quality of Prototyping:* Very efficient. Instead of asking every research team to build their own logging system (which leads to fragmentation, bugs, and inconsistent formats), a centralized middleware ensures uniformity and speed for new deployments.\n    *   *Compliance Mapping:*\n        *   Art 12(1): \"Automatic recording\" -> Checked (Middleware does this).\n        *   Art 12(2): Traceability/Post-market monitoring -> Checked (Normalized logs in time-series DB).\n        *   Art 12(3): Start/end times, reference databases, input data, human verification -> All specifically mentioned as targets for capture.\n    *   *Resource Impact:* Two senior engineers for six months is a reasonable investment to avoid repetitive engineering across multiple teams (the \"bespoke engineering\" problem). It protects the research funding envelope by centralizing the overhead.\n\n    *   The proposal is technically sound, reduces friction for prototyping other AI systems (because they just plug into the middleware), and directly addresses all legal requirements of Article 12 with a scalable architectural pattern.\n\n    *   Rationale: Centralizing logging via middleware prevents fragmented implementation across projects and ensures standardized compliance data without hindering R&D velocity. It is a technically viable, high-leverage approach that minimizes repetitive engineering overhead.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 95 (Very strong, though \"no additional hardware cost\" might be slightly optimistic depending on log volume, it's still the right architectural move).",
