mply with Article 12. P3012 doesn't *implement* Article 12; it leverages it for PR/Papers.\n    *   From a purely \"Development Engineer\" perspective focusing on \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping,\" this proposal is an academic exercise, not a technical solution to the regulatory problem.\n    *   Wait, does it *improve* the quality of prototyping? Maybe by creating better benchmarks for drift detection, which we might need for Article 12(2)(a) (identifying risks). If our logs are used to build a benchmark that then helps us detect risk in production, there's a feedback loop.\n    *   However, the primary driver here is \"publication record and credibility.\" That's not my domain of concern. My concern is whether the system *can* be built and if it works efficiently.\n    *   The proposal relies on a \"shared logging layer from the first proposal\" (which isn't provided here, but implies dependencies).\n    *   Ultimately, this is an R&D study, not an implementation of a required technical feature. In an environment of \"scarce implementation resources,\" investing 6 months into a postdoc study for papers when we have to actually *build* the high-risk system's logging capabilities seems like a distraction from the core engineering task.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* The proposal focuses on academic output rather than the technical implementation of required compliance features, diverting scarce engineering/research resources away from ensuring the baseline viability and speed of our prototyping pipeline. While technically feasible, it is a research project, not a development solution for Article 12.\n    *   *Decision:* Reject.\n    *   *Promise:* Low to Moderate (around 30). It's \"promising\" as science, but not as an engineering effort to meet a regulatory deadline.\n\n    *   Wait, the prompt asks me to judge on *my professional vantage point*. As a Dev Engineer, I care about:\n        1. Can we build it? (Yes)\n        2. Does it he