ng to rewrite the system three times because \"someone forgot we needed logs for X.\" Integrating this into the *design phase* is an engineering best practice. However, framing it as a \"peer-reviewable framework\" for high-tier journals feels more like academic prestige than raw engineering utility. But the underlying goal—treating mandated log fields as observables from day one—is technically sound and reduces risk.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* Integrating logging requirements into the initial design phase prevents costly retrospective implementation (compliance debt) and aligns technical data collection with research goals. However, it risks slowing down early-stage prototyping if the framework is too rigid.\n    *   *Decision:* Approve. (The benefit of avoiding a massive rewrite later outweighs the slight overhead in experimental setup).\n    *   *Promise:* 75. (It's very useful for long-term viability and quality, though the \"academic publication\" aspect is secondary to the engineering value).\n\n    *   Rationale: Integrating logging requirements into initial design prevents costly retrospective implementation of compliance features and streamlines the transition from prototype to production. While adding constraints early can slow some iteration, treating regulatory logs as experimental variables reduces redundant data collection efforts.\n    *   Decision: approve\n    *   Promise: 80",
