*   In an R&D context, creating the infrastructure (the \"how\") for compliance is a prerequisite for implementing high-risk systems that actually work in production without crashing performance.\n    *   *Professional Vantage Point:* As a Dev Engineer, I care about the tools my team uses. If we just \"do logging\" without a designed architecture, it'll be a mess of fragmented scripts that slow down everything. However, allocating 10% of the *entire* budget to a research track for *logging* might feel excessive or too academic (\"publish findings as open preprints\").\n    *   *Crucial Point:* The prompt says \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources... not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\" P1918 specifically uses words like \"exploring,\" \"research track,\" and \"publish findings.\" This sounds more like \"funding to explore\" than \"implementing a specific solution.\"\n    *   *Wait, reconsider:* If the lab is building high-risk AI systems, Article 12 *must* be met. It's not optional. Developing the architecture for this is an implementation necessity. But P1918 frames it as a research program to signal \"regulatory maturity\" and \"market reputation.\"\n\n    *   If I approve: I'm saying we need a dedicated, interdisciplinary team spending 10% of our budget on *researching* how to log things efficiently.\n    *   If I reject: I'm saying this is too much like an academic exercise (\"preprints\") and not enough like building the actual logging pipeline needed for current projects.\n\n    *Let's look at it from a \"speed of prototyping\" perspective:* If we don't solve the performance overhead of Article 12 now, our prototypes will be slow and unscalable. A \"Compliance-by-Design\" approach is technically sound. But does this specific proposal cross the line into \"exploratory research\" instead of \"implementation\"?\n    The phrase \"Launch an internal research track exploring...\" is very exploratory