stem; it offers to research how to build it.\n\n    Let's try to be more nuanced. Is there any reason to approve? If the \"research\" is actually \"engineering research\" (prototyping different architectures), then it fits. But the mention of \"preprints\" and \"regulatory maturity\" suggests a shift toward corporate strategy rather than development engineering.\n\n    *Final decision path:*\n    Rationale: While performance-neutral logging is technically necessary to maintain prototyping speed, this proposal is framed as an exploratory research program focused on academic signaling rather than an implementation roadmap for required infrastructure.\n    Decision: reject.\n    Promise: 40 (The technical goal is correct, but the proposed vehicle—a research track with preprints—is not what a Dev Engineer needs to get systems running).\n\n    *Wait*, looking at the prompt again: \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources... not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\"\n    P1918: \"Fund a... Research Programme. Launch an internal research track exploring...\"\n    This is almost the definition of what I am told *not* to approve if it's just exploration.\n\n    Let me refine the rationale one last time for clarity and professional tone.",
