ifecycle* (Recital 71). It does not address the *logging requirements* (Article 12). But the prompt asks to screen based on the provided text of Article 12/Recital 71. Recital 71 specifically mentions technical documentation for algorithms, data, etc.\n\n    Pros: Ensures reproducibility and traceability (high engineering quality), prevents \"scrambling\" at the end.\n    Cons: Administrative overhead, potential bottleneck in prototyping speed.\n\n    *Decision:* Approve. While bureaucratic, a centralized mapping of hyperparameters and weights is an engineering best practice (MLOps) that supports high-quality prototyping and ensures the system can actually be released. The \"CTO escalation\" is annoying but doesn't break technical viability.\n\n    *Rationale:* This establishes the necessary MLOps discipline for traceability and reproducibility, though the administrative overhead may slightly impede prototyping speed. It directly addresses the documentation requirements of Recital 71, which is a prerequisite for the legal viability of any high-risk system we develop.\n    *Decision:* approve.\n    *Promise:* 75 (High value for quality/compliance, moderate friction for speed).\n\n    Wait, let me re-read the persona: \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\"\n    If I am a *Dev Engineer*, do I want this? If I don't have a way to track my experiments, my prototypes are low quality. This proposal forces that tracking. It's essentially an MLOps mandate.\n\n    Let's check for any \"deal-breakers\".\n    \"Quarterly documentation-audit cycle tied to each version release.\" -> In R&D, we might have 10 version releases a week. If every one triggers an audit, it's a disaster. But it says \"quarterly... tied to each version release,\" which is slightly contradictory or implies audits happen quarterly *on* the latest releases.\n    \"Central register that maps every system component—model weights, dataset provenance, hyperparameters...\" -> This i